# Standards: Core
> Universal. Language-agnostic. Tool-agnostic. Stack-agnostic.
> Every project in the factory inherits this.
> References to specific languages, frameworks, or tools belong ONLY in stack standards.

---

## 1. Language Rules

### Code — Always English (no exceptions)
- Variable names, function names, class names, type names, enum values
- Comments and inline documentation
- Error messages, log messages, exception messages
- Database table names, column names, migration file names
- Configuration keys and environment variable names
- Test names and test file descriptions

### Technical documentation — Always English
- PRD, ARCHITECTURE, SPEC, api-spec, data-model, ADRs
- README, guides, runbooks, changelogs

### Communication — Configurable per project
Read `language.communication` from `.hive/AGENTS.local.md`.
Use that language for:
- Agent responses in chat
- Commit messages, PR titles and descriptions
- Ticket descriptions and acceptance criteria
- Script menus and interactive prompts

When communication language is Spanish, always use **neutral Spanish** (no regional expressions).

---

## 2. Naming Conventions

| Artifact | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Classes, types, interfaces | PascalCase | `EmployeeService`, `OrderRepository` |
| Functions, methods, variables | camelCase | `findByEmail`, `isActive` |
| Constants | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | `MAX_RETRIES`, `DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE` |
| Files — class/component | PascalCase | `EmployeeService.ts`, `PositionCard.tsx` |
| Files — utility/config | camelCase | `dateUtils.ts`, `appConfig.py` |
| Directories | kebab-case | `employee-service/`, `order-management/` |
| Environment variables | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | `DATABASE_URL`, `API_SECRET_KEY` |

**Notes:**
- Some languages have established conventions that differ (e.g. Python uses `snake_case` for functions/variables). In those cases, **follow the language convention** for code, but apply the above for files, directories, env vars, and all non-code artifacts.
- Boolean names: `is*`, `has*`, `can*`, `should*` — never bare adjectives
- Collection names: plural noun (`employees`, `orders`) — never `list`, `data`, `stuff`
- Functions: verb + noun (`createEmployee`, `validateEmail`, `find_by_email`)

